From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:59:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] AROS: names in the system's character set
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Repository: https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch
Base: fastfetch 2.70.0, 17bb3fd2384bd814b421d73b8c8b8d08e06cfa26
Problem: the AROS backend took every name the system keeps (volumes,
devices, drawers, fonts, screen modes, tasks, locale names) as ISO-8859-1
and wrote CON: in ISO-8859-1. AROS stores such names in the 8-bit set the
user chose in Locale prefs: with ISO-8859-2 the byte of a Polish "ą" came
out as "±", in JSON and on screen. The program drawer's name, converted for
display, was also used to open presets beside the program, so a drawer
with a non-ASCII name was not found.
Solution: the set comes from codesets.library (the system default it
reports, which follows ENV:CHARSET), read once as a table of the bytes
0x80..0xFF; ISO-8859-1 when the library is missing. Names convert through
that table to UTF-8 (U+FFFD for a byte without a character; a UTF-8
system passes valid UTF-8). CON: and other consoles that do not answer
the Limpet query get text back in the same set; a terminal that says it
does not decode UTF-8 gets Latin-1, as its protocol defines. Characters
the set lacks become the nearest ASCII (dashes, quotes, bar and box
characters), else "?". Presets beside the program are opened through
PROGDIR:, not through the converted name.
Scope: common/aros/aros_charset.c and .h (new), aros_text.h, aros_output.c,
aros_terminal.c and .h, the AROS detection files that convert names,
fastfetch.c (preset lookup, AROS only), CMakeLists.txt (the new file).
Upstream-Status: AROS only; part of the AROS backend.
Verification: host tests in tests/test_port.py (Latin-2 and Latin-1 names,
an ISO-8859-2 console, UTF-8 systems, ill-formed bytes); cross-built for
the three targets. Guest runs: docs/evidence/2026-10-27-r2/.
Removal: with the AROS backend.
